{#- Atlas — surface two: triage on a phone (decision 27), grouped by state (design doc: 1c).

  Presentation only. src/triage.mjs still decides everything: which state a workstream is in, and
  the order both this page and state.json read. This layout adds section headers over groups that
  were always contiguous in `triaged` — it classifies nothing and sorts nothing.

  Section-boundary detection: `{% set currentState = stream.triage %}` reassigns the outer-scope
  scalar from inside the loop (nested inside an `{% if %}`, itself inside the `{% for %}`), and
  Nunjucks 3.2.4 — the version pinned in package.json, arriving via @11ty/eleventy — does carry
  that reassignment forward into later iterations. Verified with a scratch template before this
  file was written, because "Nunjucks can't mutate an outer-scope variable inside a for loop" reads
  like it should forbid exactly this pattern.

  The per-section count deliberately does NOT use `triaged | selectattr("triage", "equalto", ...)`.
  That scratch check also turned up a real bug in this pinned Nunjucks: `selectattr`'s filter
  implementation (theirs, not ours) ignores the test name and comparison value entirely and just
  returns every item whose attribute is truthy — so it would report the same total workstream count
  on every heading, not each section's own count. `triaged | groupby("triage")` plus a dynamic
  subscript (`groups[currentState] | length`) does not go through that broken path.

  Data this layout expects:
    triaged — orderByTriage(...)'s output: the workstreams already in card order, each one a
              { slug, manifest, triage, column }, where `triage` is its state and `column` is its
              own entry from computeLadder's columns — including `covered`, `completedCount` and
              `milestoneCount`, which are what this page draws the track from
    project, repo, title — see base.njk
-#}
{% extends "base.njk" %}
{% import "base.njk" as ui %}

{% block bodyClass %}page page-mobile{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
<h1>What needs you</h1>
<p class="lede">Grouped by what's blocking, not by feature. Every card ends on what's next for it.</p>

{%- set sectionCounts = triaged | groupby("triage") %}
{%- set currentState = "" %}
{%- for stream in triaged %}
{%- if stream.triage != currentState %}
{%- if not loop.first %}</ul>{% endif %}
{%- set currentState = stream.triage %}
<h2 class="triage-heading">{{ ui.chipLabel(currentState) }} <span class="triage-count">{{ sectionCounts[currentState] | length }}</span></h2>
<ul class="card-list">
{%- endif %}
<li>
<article class="card" data-workstream="{{ stream.manifest.codename }}" data-triage="{{ stream.triage }}">
<header class="card-head">
<h3><a href="{{ stream.url }}">{{ stream.manifest.codename }}</a></h3>
</header>
<p class="card-what">{{ stream.manifest.what }}</p>
<p class="card-position">{{ stream.manifest.position }}</p>
{#- How much of the track is filled. Not counted here: `computeLadder` decides what "complete"
    means for a workstream, and the feature planning drawing fills its ribbon from the same flags.
    This page reads them. A second count here is exactly how these two surfaces came to state
    contradictory facts about one workstream.

    "Complete" means THIS MILESTONE IS FINISHED — every `done` one, wherever it sits. M1 counted
    the contiguous run from depth 1, so the first milestone that was not done stopped the count
    dead; #780 reversed that, because a feature that parked one milestone and shipped four more
    after it is four complete, not none. A milestone the work went ROUND is drawn on the desk view
    as a skip marker and is not counted here, which is what makes "4 of 5" the honest reading. -#}
{% if stream.column.milestoneCount %}
<div class="track" role="img" aria-label="{{ stream.column.completedCount }} of {{ stream.column.milestoneCount }} milestones complete">
{% for filled in stream.column.covered %}<span class="track-seg{% if filled %} is-filled{% endif %}"></span>{% endfor %}
</div>
<p class="track-count"><span class="num">{{ stream.column.completedCount }}</span> of <span class="num">{{ stream.column.milestoneCount }}</span> milestones complete</p>
{% else %}
<p class="track-count empty">No milestones on record yet</p>
{% endif %}
{#- Only when there IS one. `tipLabel` is null when nothing is on record for this feature to do
    next, and #780 found the alternative rendered: this line read "Next: M5" for a feature with
    four milestones, all done and nothing beyond, while the desk chart correctly drew no balloon
    for it. The two surfaces read this one field now, so they cannot say different things. -#}
{% if stream.column.tipLabel %}<p class="card-next">Next: {{ stream.column.tipLabel }}</p>{% endif %}
<p class="card-status"><span class="card-status-label">Status</span>{{ stream.manifest.next }}</p>
</article>
</li>
{%- if loop.last %}</ul>{% endif %}
{%- endfor %}
{% endblock %}
